# Copy this to .env and fill in the values.
#
# Everything here can ALSO be set in your compose file's `environment:` block instead, and a
# value there wins over the same name here. Use whichever suits you - .env keeps your slskd API
# key out of a file you might paste into a forum post, while `environment:` keeps everything
# about the container in one place. Mixing them is fine and deadwax logs which source each
# setting came from on startup.
#
# One thing not to do: `SLSKD_URL=${SLSKD_URL}` in `environment:`. Compose interpolates that
# from its own environment, and an empty result still counts as set - so it beats this file and
# leaves the setting blank while the line below looks perfectly correct. Write literal values
# there. See docker-compose.example.yml.
#
# Keep the examples on their own lines like this rather than trailing the value
# (SLSKD_URL=   #http://...). Docker Compose and python-dotenv disagree about inline
# comments, and a blank value with a trailing comment is a good way to end up with an
# empty setting and a confusing error.

# Run as this user/group rather than root. Match these to slskd and your other *arr
# containers so everything writes files with consistent ownership - `id yourname` on the
# host prints the values you want. Files the organizer creates will be owned by this user.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000

# Your email address, for MusicBrainz: they ask every app for a contact, and throttle the ones
# that don't send one. deadwax writes the rest of its user agent itself, version included:
# deadwax/<version> ( you@example.com ). (An old hand-written MUSICBRAINZ_USERAGENT still
# works, but this replaces it.)
MUSICBRAINZ_EMAIL=

# full URL including the scheme - http://slskd:5030, NOT slskd:5030
SLSKD_URL=

SLSKD_APIKEY=

# off      - never organize, downloads just stay in slskd's folder
# dry_run  - log what would happen, touch nothing  (DEFAULT)
# copy     - copy into the library, leave slskd's copy alone
# move     - move into the library
#
# starts at dry_run on purpose: organizing is the only thing here that writes to your
# filesystem, so check the event log looks right before switching to copy or move.
ORGANIZE_MODE=dry_run

# on | off - look up lyrics on LRCLIB for each album as it is filed, saved as a .lrc beside
# every track. Only adds files, and a .lrc already there is never replaced. Also changeable
# in the settings tab, and 'Get lyrics' in the library works either way.
#FETCH_LYRICS=on
#
# milliseconds to move synced lyrics EARLIER as they are written (negative moves them later).
# LRCLIB's timings are tapped along by people and trail the singing a little - if lines land a
# beat late in your player, try 300. Re-time lyrics already saved from the settings tab.
#LYRICS_LEAD_MS=0

# on | off - whether a release's country names its folder when nothing else tells the
# pressing apart: Dummy (1994) [GB]. Off by default. Two different pressings still get their
# own folders either way, by catalogue number.
#COUNTRY_IN_FOLDER=off

# how an ALBUM folder is named - the artist folder above it is always the artist. Tokens:
# {album} {artist} {year} {release_year} {edition} {format} {country} {catalog}; a token left
# empty takes its brackets with it. Empty is the default, which is exactly:
#ALBUM_FOLDER_TEMPLATE={album} ({year}) [{edition}]

# on | off - when a download fails, move it to the next peer from the list you picked it from,
# by itself. Off by default: the next peer down may be a different pressing or a worse rip.
# "Try next peer" on a failed download in the downloads panel works either way.
#AUTO_RETRY_PEER=off

# seconds each Soulseek search listens for answers, 3 to 60. Peers answer over several
# seconds, slow and firewalled ones last - longer finds more, shorter answers sooner.
#SLSKD_SEARCH_TIMEOUT=8

# seconds between writing an album's new tags and renaming its folder, when applying a release
# changes both - so Navidrome keeps the album's plays, ratings and favourites, which it drops
# when both change at once. With Navidrome set up below, deadwax asks it when it has scanned
# instead of counting, and this only has to be more than 0. 0 renames straight away. 0 to 300.
#RETAG_RENAME_WAIT=20

# Optional - the player at /player/, which plays your library from Navidrome. deadwax keeps
# this login and passes the player's requests on, so the phone never sees the password and only
# ever needs to reach deadwax. With it set, applying a release also asks Navidrome when it has
# scanned before renaming a folder.
#
# The URL is Navidrome's address as seen from inside THIS container, with the scheme - plus
# Navidrome's base path if it has one (ND_BASEPATH), as in http://navidrome:4533/music, with no
# /rest on the end: deadwax adds that itself. Changing it later in the settings tab takes the
# password again.
#
# A non-admin account of your own is enough - nothing the player asks for needs an admin. The
# password is better kept here than in compose, like the slskd key.
#NAVIDROME_URL=http://navidrome:4533
#NAVIDROME_USER=
#NAVIDROME_PASSWORD=

# The three below are set for you in docker-compose.example.yml and match the volume
# mounts there - you only need them if you're running outside of that compose file.
#
# where slskd puts completed downloads, as seen from inside THIS container. Must point at
# the same files slskd writes to.
#SLSKD_DOWNLOAD_PATH=/downloads
#
# where organized music gets filed to
#LIBRARY_PATH=/music
#
# slskd's INCOMPLETE folder, as seen from inside this container. Optional, and cleanup is off
# without it: set it and cancelling a download also removes the half-finished file, and the
# empty folders slskd leaves in there are cleared every ten minutes; leave it and both stay. slskd keeps partials on purpose - with retry.partial set to Resume it
# picks a retried download up from where the partial ended instead of starting again - so only
# set this if a cancelled download is one you're finished with.
#SLSKD_INCOMPLETE_PATH=/incomplete
#
# where the database lives, needs to be on a persistent volume
#DB_PATH=/config/deadwax.db
